course: dictionary
lesson: 15163
title: Villains and scoundrels
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A scoundrel is a wicked, dishonest person who cheats or does bad things to other people. Look at the clues in the sentence: «The scoundrel stole the old lady's purse and ran off.» Stealing from someone and running away tells you a scoundrel is a bad person.
explain: Scoundrel is an old-fashioned word. You will mostly meet it in older stories, plays and cartoons, where a hero might shout, «You scoundrel!» at the villain who has tricked him. Modern writers and speakers use it less often, so context helps you spot what it means.
explain: In The Wind in the Willows, the weasels and stoats who seize Toad Hall are called villains and scoundrels. The words tell you they are the wicked characters in the story, not the friendly animals like Mole and Ratty.
fact: Scoundrel is a noun: a scoundrel, scoundrels.
fact: «Scoundrel» means a wicked, dishonest person who cheats or does bad things to others.
fact: Scoundrel is old-fashioned, so it is found more often in stories and plays than in everyday talk.
fact: A villain is a wicked character in a story. A scoundrel is a dishonest person who does bad things, and the two words often appear together.
fact: In The Wind in the Willows, the weasels and stoats are called scoundrels, and Toad is called an incorrigible rogue and a hardened ruffian.
exercise: «The scoundrel stole the old lady's purse and ran off.» What does scoundrel mean? | A small bird that sings at dawn | A friendly old dog | A kind person who helps lost children | *A wicked, dishonest person who does bad things to others
exercise: «You scoundrel! You broke your promise to me.» What is the speaker feeling? | Bored and sleepy | *Angry with someone who cheated them | Surprised by a present | Very happy and thankful
exercise: «The scoundrel cheated the children out of their pocket money.» Who is the scoundrel in this sentence? | The sweet shop that opened early | *The person who cheated the children | The children who lost their money | The teacher who gave them a prize
test: «You scoundrel!» is a phrase you would most likely hear in which kind of text? | A report of a football match | *An older story, play or cartoon with a villain | A weather report on the television | A recipe for pancakes
test: In The Wind in the Willows, who are called villains and scoundrels? | Toad's washing and his car | The badger who keeps the peace | *The weasels and stoats who seize Toad Hall | Mole and Ratty
exam: «The magistrate called Toad an incorrigible rogue and a hardened ruffian.» Which word in the sentence means about the same as scoundrel? | sunshine | breakfast | whisper | *rogue
